---
// The Outcomes section (feat-157): a row of 2-3 big figures saying what the
// work changed. Figures and labels only — nothing else.
//
// It shipped with a visible method line under the figures (n, window,
// source) and Max cut it in review: at 14px across three lines it read as a
// slab of small coloured text under the numbers. It is not restylable on
// this palette either — the only token less saturated than the figures'
// --color-text-primary is --color-text-emphasis, which is BRIGHTER than
// them, so demoting the note by colour would have promoted it instead. The
// method is now a `// Method:` comment above each config's outcomes block;
// see .context/identity.md Product Principle #2, which requires it to be
// recorded rather than rendered.
//
// Renders second on every page, directly under Overview and above
// Challenge (moved there 2026-09-15; it sat after Deliverables first). So a
// case study now opens "here is the product, here is what it changed", and
// only then walks the problem and the process.
//
// That is deliberately results-first rather than narrative order: a hiring
// manager skimming gets the numbers without scrolling, which is the whole
// reason the section exists. The cost is that the figures arrive before the
// problem that frames them, which the labels have to carry on their own —
// it is why they read "Faster triage, 7.9 -> 2.4 min" and not "Faster".
//
// Deliberately NOT a new visual language: this is entities/stat, the same
// component BuildSection already runs, lifted into its own section. No new
// type ramp, no new spacing values, no new colour.
//
// ── Sizing, all of it measured ───────────────────────────────────────────
// Two figures per page, max (copy-048 — three read as too much at this
// scale). Three constraints fix the grid, and each is a number off the real
// render, not a round guess:
//
//   * The widest figure any page carries is defenceSystems's "240%", 219px intrinsic
//     (Manrope Medium 96px, tabular-nums, -1.92px tracking, plus the 32px
//     unit). A track narrower than that clips it.
//   * The narrowest body a two-column row has to survive is 602px, at a
//     1024px viewport — just above where .section-grid drops its 33/66
//     split. Two 230px tracks plus the 40px gap need 500px, which fits.
//     Raise the floor past ~280px and 1024-1100px viewports start wrapping.
//   * Below a 580px viewport the body is under 500px and it goes to one
//     column on its own, which is what a phone wants anyway.
//
// So 230px is the window between "holds the widest figure" and "still fits
// two columns on a small laptop". Both ends are tight; neither is arbitrary.
//
// Labels are written to one line at the 316px track a 1280px viewport
// gives (see the trims in copy-048) — every one of the 24 measures 315px or
// less. At 1100px and below they wrap to two, which is why `text-wrap:
// balance` is here rather than left to the greedy default.
//
// ── Why no +/- signs on the figures ───────────────────────────────────────
// A signed figure is ~56px wider at this ramp ("+118%" measures 272px
// against ~216px unsigned). Direction lives in the label instead ("More
// course enquiries…", "Fewer P1 tickets…"), which also reads better: the
// label already carries the before -> after, so the sign was never the thing
// telling the reader which way the number moved.
import { Stat } from '@entities/stat';
import { Heading } from '@shared/ui';
import type { OutcomesData } from '@entities/project';

interface Props {
  heading: string;
  stats: OutcomesData['stats'];
}

const { heading, stats } = Astro.props;
---

<section class="section-grid outcomes-section">
  <Heading class="section-grid__aside outcomes-section__heading">{heading}</Heading>
  <div class="section-grid__body outcomes-section__stats" data-reveal>
    {
      stats.map((stat) => (
        <Stat value={stat.value} unit={stat.unit} label={stat.label} />
      ))
    }
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* Grid, column spans and the 1023px collapse come from .section-grid in
     global.css (ref-005). The stats grid IS the section body — there is no
     second child to stack under it. */
  .outcomes-section__stats {
    display: grid;
    /* 230px is derived, not chosen — see the header comment for the three
       measurements that pin it. */
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(230px, 1fr));
    /* 40px column gap matches BuildSection's row exactly. The row gap only
       applies below a 580px viewport, where the row is a single column. */
    gap: var(--spacing-xl) 40px;
  }
  /* Evens the two lines instead of filling line one and orphaning whatever
     is left. Only bites below a 1280px viewport, where labels start wrapping
     — above that every label is a single line by construction. Scoped to
     these labels rather than entities/stat, because BuildSection's are short
     enough to stay on one line at any width and balancing one line is a
     no-op. Unsupported browsers get the greedy wrap this replaces. */
  :global(.outcomes-section__stats .stat__label) {
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    /* No `flex-direction: column` here, unlike BuildSection's row — the grid
       above collapsed to one track back at ~580px. This only closes the gap
       up, since a stacked column does not need the 32px a wrapped row does. */
    .outcomes-section__stats {
      gap: var(--spacing-lg);
    }
  }
</style>
